# Azure for {{.Repo}} (aayudha)

Written by `aayu init --integrations` (WP7.2b). aayudha reads Application Insights through the Log Analytics
query API with a read-only identity, and, optionally, keeps proof media in a private Blob Storage container.
GitHub Actions gets short-lived tokens through OpenID Connect federation; no key is stored.

## Founder actions (read-only analytics)

Run these with an account that may create app registrations and role assignments.

1. Note the IDs:

   ```sh
   az account show --query '{tenant: tenantId, subscription: id}'
   az monitor app-insights component show --app <component> --resource-group <group> --query '{id: id, workspace: workspaceResourceId}'
   ```

2. Create the read-only app registration and its service principal:

   ```sh
   az ad app create --display-name aayudha-read-{{.Name}} --query appId -o tsv   # the client ID
   az ad sp create --id <client-id>
   ```

3. Trust this repository's `{{.Branch}}` branch on GitHub Actions (no client secret):

   ```sh
   az ad app federated-credential create --id <client-id> --parameters .aayudha/setup/azure/federated-credential.json
   ```

4. Grant read only, on the component and on its Log Analytics workspace:

   ```sh
   az role assignment create --assignee <client-id> --role "Monitoring Reader" --scope <component id>
   az role assignment create --assignee <client-id> --role "Log Analytics Reader" --scope <workspace id>
   ```

   No other role. aayudha asks Azure Resource Manager for the identity's actions on the resource before every
   query and refuses one that can write.

5. Fill `[integrations.azure]` in `.aayudha/config.toml`: `org` = the tenant ID, `identity` = the client ID,
   `project` = the component ID. Commit it through a pull request.

6. In each workflow job that runs `aayu` with it, add the snippet in `workflow.yml` (`id-token: write`).

7. Check: `aayu doctor` (in Actions, or locally as below) reports `azure: reachable as application …;
   read-only scope verified`.

Locally, `auth = "oidc"` has no GitHub token to exchange. Run with your `az login` instead, which must itself
be read-only on the resource (doctor refuses an Owner or Contributor login):

```sh
AAYU_INTEGRATIONS__AZURE__AUTH=cli aayu doctor
```

## Founder actions (proof media, optional)

A public repository needs a private store for proof media (V3). Use a separate identity from the read-only one.

1. Create a storage account with public access off and a private container (`az storage account create
   --allow-blob-public-access false …`, `az storage container create --name proof …`).
2. Either create a container SAS token with read, create, write, delete, and list (`sp=rcwdl`) and an expiry,
   and store it with `aayu auth set-secret proof-media`; or give an Entra identity "Storage Blob Data
   Contributor" on the container.
3. Set `[proof] media = "azblob://<account>/proof/{{.Name}}"` (add `?auth=oidc&tenant=…&client=…` for an Entra
   identity).
4. A SAS token cannot manage the account's lifecycle policy: apply `lifecycle-policy.json` once
   (`az storage account management-policy create --account-name <account> --resource-group <group> --policy
   @.aayudha/setup/azure/lifecycle-policy.json`), after replacing `<container>`. With an Entra identity that
   may write `Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts/managementPolicies`, add `&subscription=…&group=…` to the media
   URL and aayudha keeps the rule itself.
